/*
================================================================================
  ABANDONED REBUILD -- NOT PART OF THE MODEL SUITE.  Kept so the next attempt
  starts where this one stopped rather than from scratch.  It does NOT run
  under models/run-all.sh and is not claimed to prove anything: the two
  headline lemmas do not converge.  See Robot/review-tracking.md, 2026-09-05.
================================================================================
*/

theory RHTNCurrency
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN currency attestation + stapling -- symbolic analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  design Section 12.6.5 replaces revocation with short-lived patron-signed
  CURRENCY ATTESTATIONS, stapled to introductions.  The review plan's Stage
  1.1 target: "a revoked key cannot be made to appear current beyond the
  attestation lifetime".  The property proved here: a relying party accepts
  a key as current only if the patron signed an attestation for it that has
  not expired -- so once the patron rotates the subject to a new key and the
  old attestation's lifetime passes, no attestation the adversary can
  assemble makes the OLD key look current.

  This model also exercises the 2026-08-6 "issue fresh, never extend stale"
  rule and the fail-open/fail-closed split (design Section 12.6.5): the
  lemma is about TRUST-BEARING acceptance, which fails closed, not routine
  payload, which does not.

TIME, SYMBOLICALLY
  Tamarin has no clock.  We model expiry as EVENT ORDER using action facts
  and a restriction, the standard idiom (as in the plan's note that "expiry
  is event ordering, not clocks"):
    * an attestation is minted with an Issued action carrying an epoch term;
    * an Expire action can fire for an epoch;
    * a restriction forbids ACCEPTING an attestation whose epoch has expired
      before the acceptance.
  "Epoch" is an abstract token, not a number -- we need only the ordering
  "issued, then (maybe) expired, then someone tries to rely on it".

  Reading conventions (rule shape, fact sorts, ~/$ prefixes, the trace,
  temporal logic) are spelled out in attach.spthy; only NEW idioms are
  re-explained here.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITIES AND KEYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A patron and the subjects it vouches for.  The patron has a long-term
  signing key; a subject is represented by a KEY it currently holds.  A
  rotation gives the subject a new key and (per the design) the patron will
  issue attestations for the new key, not the old.
*/

rule Register_Patron:
    [ Fr(~psk) ]
  --[ PatronReg($P), OnlyOnce($P) ]->
    [ !PatronKey($P, ~psk)             // the patron's long-term secret key
    , !Pk($P, pk(~psk))
    , Out(pk(~psk)) ]

// A subject's key.  The `key` term ~sk stands for the whole key material
// the keyhash commits to; $S is the subject identity.  A subject may hold
// several keys over time (rotation); each is a distinct fresh value.
rule Subject_Key:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ SubjectKey($S, ~sk) ]->
    [ !SubjKey($S, ~sk)                // a key $S holds (persistent)
    , Out(pk(~sk)) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHO HOLDS WHICH KEY AS CURRENT -- PER PARTY, NEVER GLOBALLY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design Section 9.0.2 [author, 2026-09-05]: a recovery adoption reaching a
  node inside the horizon makes it "remove the old key from its records and
  overwrite it with the new one", while "any node outside of the horizon
  should continue seeing old and new keys as separate entities".

  SO THERE IS NO GLOBAL CURRENT KEY, and this model must not invent one.
  design Section 16.1's per-observer rule and the design's no-shared-state
  rule both forbid it, and an earlier sketch that used one linear fact for
  "the" current key was modelling a network-wide register that does not
  exist.  `View(A, S, k)` is A's OWN record and nobody else's.

  WHETHER A PARTY RECEIVES THE ADOPTION IS THE ADVERSARY'S CHOICE, which is
  both the right Dolev-Yao shape and the right RHTN one: a party that never
  receives it simply keeps its old view, and that is exactly what being
  outside the horizon looks like from here.  No horizon predicate is needed
  or wanted.
*/

// A party's first record of a subject.  One per (party, subject) pair.
rule Learn_Subject:
    [ !SubjKey($S, sk) ]
  --[ FirstView($A, $S), ViewIs($A, $S, sk) ]->
    [ View($A, $S, sk) ]

restriction OneFirstViewPerPair:
  "All a s #i #j. FirstView(a, s) @ #i & FirstView(a, s) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// The recovery adoption itself: it names the prior key and the successor
// (`wire-format.md` Section 4.1 field 6).  ROTATE-BACK IS FORBIDDEN --
// "prior_key MUST differ from field 1, identical keys represent no
// rotation" -- which is also what keeps the search below finite.
// THE SUCCESSOR IS FRESH KEY MATERIAL, never a key the subject already
// held.  design Section 9.1 rotates "the old identity to a NEW keypair",
// and `wire-format.md` Section 4.1 adds that "prior_key MUST differ from
// field 1 -- identical keys represent no rotation".  Drawing the successor
// with Fr() rather than picking an existing key is what makes the successor
// relation acyclic: a key that has been superseded can never become current
// again, which a rotate-back chain would otherwise allow and which is also
// what kept an earlier version of this search from terminating.
rule Recovery_Adoption:
    [ !SubjKey($S, old)
    , Fr(~new) ]
  --[ RotationExists($S, old, ~new), SuccessorFresh($S, ~new)
    , OneRotationPer($S) ]->
    [ !SubjKey($S, ~new)
    , !Adoption($S, old, ~new)
    , Out(<'adopt', $S, old, ~new>)
    , Out(pk(~new)) ]

// A party receives the adoption and OVERWRITES its own record.  The old key
// is gone from THIS party's view; other parties are untouched.
rule Receive_Adoption:
    [ View($A, $S, old)
    , !Adoption($S, old, new) ]
  --[ Overwrote($A, $S, old, new), ViewIs($A, $S, new) ]->
    [ View($A, $S, new) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EPOCHS -- the abstract clock
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A fresh epoch token is drawn to stamp each attestation.  An epoch may
  later Expire.  The ORDER of Issued / Expire / Accept actions on the trace
  is the entire content of "lifetime": we never compare durations, only
  whether an expiry preceded an acceptance.
*/

rule New_Epoch:
    [ Fr(~e) ]
  --[ EpochStart(~e) ]->
    [ !Epoch(~e), Out(~e) ]            // epochs are public tokens

rule Expire_Epoch:
    [ !Epoch(~e) ]
  --[ Expired(~e) ]->                  // trace: epoch ~e is now past
    [ ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ISSUING AN ATTESTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The patron signs <'currency', subject, subjectKey, epoch>.  Binding the
  SUBJECT KEY into the signature is the crux: an attestation vouches for a
  specific key (design Section 12.6.5's `current_key` field), so it cannot
  be transplanted onto a different key.  Binding the EPOCH lets expiry bite.

  "Issue fresh, never extend stale" (design Section 12.6.5.1) is modelled by
  every issuance drawing the CURRENT epoch: the patron cannot re-stamp an
  old attestation with a new epoch without signing a new one, and signing a
  new one is exactly issuing fresh.
*/

rule Issue_Currency:
    [ !PatronKey($P, ~psk)             // only the patron can issue
    , View($P, $S, sk)                 // for the key IT records as current
    , !Epoch(e) ]                      // stamped with an epoch
  --[ Issued($P, $S, sk, e)
    , ViewHeld($P, $S, sk) ]->           // stamped: every restoration of the
                                       //   view says what it restored, so a
                                       //   proof need not chain backwards
                                       //   through issuances to find out
    [ View($P, $S, sk)                 // restored: issuing is not a rotation
    , Out(sign(<'currency', $S, sk, e>, ~psk)) ]  // the stapled attestation

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  RELYING ON AN ATTESTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A relying party performs a TRUST-BEARING operation: it accepts subject $S
  as current under key sk, having checked a purported attestation from
  patron $P.  Two things gate the acceptance:
    * the signature verifies (the Eq restriction, as in attach.spthy);
    * the epoch has NOT expired before this acceptance (the NotExpired
      restriction below) -- this is the fail-CLOSED behaviour for
      trust-bearing operations (design Section 12.6.5).
*/

rule Accept_Currency:
    // The staple arrives carrying its fields AND the signature over them
    // (design Section 12.6.5: the introducing node "staples a recent
    // patron-signed currency attestation to its introduction").  Taking the
    // fields from the message is also what BINDS them: an earlier version
    // read `sk` from nowhere, which Tamarin reports as an unbound variable
    // and unintended pattern matching.
    [ In(<'currency', $S, sk, e, att>) // the stapled attestation, as received
    , !Pk($P, pkP)                     // the patron's public key
    , !Epoch(e) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(att, <'currency', $S, sk, e>, pkP), true)
    , NotExpiredAt(e)                  // guarded by the restriction below
    , Accepted($P, $S, sk, e) ]->      // trace: relied on sk as current
    [ ]

/*
  ONE REGISTRATION PER NAME.  A public label like $A stands for a keyhash,
  and design Section 5.1 makes a keyhash a commitment to the key material --
  change the material and you have a different identity.  Without the
  restriction below, Register could fire twice for one label and give a
  single symbolic name two unrelated keys, so "the party I meant to reach"
  would not pin a key.  (currency.spthy's Subject_Key is deliberately NOT
  restricted this way: a subject holding several keys over time is rotation,
  which is that theory's subject matter.)
*/

/*
  A BOUNDED INSTANCE, stated rather than hidden.  One rotation per subject.
  Unbounded rotation chains -- each fresh successor enabling another --
  make the search below diverge, and a bounded instance is how every model
  in this directory works: the TLA+ theories run three nodes, not all
  networks.  ONE rotation is enough to state and test supersession, because
  the design's claim is about a key being replaced, not about the length of
  a chain of replacements.  What is proved here therefore holds for
  instances with a single rotation per subject, and that is the honest
  scope of the result.
*/
restriction OneRotationPerSubject:
  "All s #i #j. OneRotationPer(s) @ #i & OneRotationPer(s) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction OneKeyPerName:
  "All a #i #j. OnlyOnce(a) @ #i & OnlyOnce(a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

// The fail-closed rule: an acceptance stamped NotExpiredAt(e) is only
// allowed on traces where no Expired(e) happened BEFORE it.  This is how
// "current vs expired" becomes event order without a clock.
//
// THIS IS AN AXIOM, NOT A RESULT, and the difference matters when reading
// the lemma below.  A restriction DISCARDS traces; it does not show the
// protocol prevents them.  What is modelled here is a relying party with a
// trustworthy clock that refuses an expired staple -- an environment
// assumption, exactly like the co-presence axiom in ceremony.spthy.  So
// currency_requires_unexpired_issuance holds *relative to* clock
// enforcement; it is not evidence that clock enforcement happens.  Nor does
// expiry_is_reachable establish that every issued attestation eventually
// expires -- only that an expiry can occur, so the restriction is not
// vacuously satisfied by nothing ever expiring.
restriction NotExpiredBeforeAccept:
  "All e #a. NotExpiredAt(e) @ #a
     ==> not(Ex #x. Expired(e) @ #x & #x < #a)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// SANITY: the honest flow runs -- a patron issues, a party accepts.
lemma currency_is_usable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S sk e #i. Accepted(P, S, sk, e) @ #i"

// SANITY: an epoch can actually expire, so the NotExpiredBeforeAccept
// restriction is not vacuously satisfiable by nothing ever expiring.
// NOTE WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY: it asserts only that Expired(e) occurs.  It
// does not exhibit an acceptance alongside it, and an earlier comment here
// claimed it did.
lemma expiry_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex e #x. Expired(e) @ #x"

// THE SECURITY PROPERTY (Stage 1.1).  Every trust-bearing acceptance of a
// key sk as current rests on a genuine, unexpired patron issuance for that
// exact key -- OR the patron's key was compromised.  Because Accept is
// gated by NotExpiredBeforeAccept, an EXPIRED attestation cannot be
// accepted; because the signature binds sk, an attestation for one key
// cannot be replayed onto another.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE, AND THE MODEL CANNOT CURRENTLY EXPRESS.  There
// is no notion of a CURRENT key here: !SubjKey and !Epoch are both
// persistent, so a patron can mint a BRAND-NEW attestation for a key the
// subject rotated away from, stamped with an epoch that began after the
// successor existed.  Tamarin confirms the trace exists with no compromise.
// So this lemma establishes only that an accepted (S,sk,e) was signed for
// that same (S,sk,e) and had not expired -- NOT that sk is current.
//
// design Section 12.6.5 keeps two rules apart and warns against conflating
// them: "Expiry bounds what a stolen credential can spend; supersession,
// once known, is what retires it from use." This model has the first and
// not the second, which is exactly the conflation that section forbids.
// Closing it needs a linear current-key binding that rotation consumes,
// plus a relying party that can hold supersession evidence -- a rebuild
// rather than a patch, and open work rather than a silent limitation.
lemma currency_requires_unexpired_issuance:
  "All P S sk e #i.
     Accepted(P, S, sk, e) @ #i
     ==> ( ( (Ex #j. Issued(P, S, sk, e) @ #j & #j < #i)
           & not(Ex #x. Expired(e) @ #x & #x < #i) )
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(P) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

// PATRON COMPROMISE, made available so the carve-out above is real rather
// than decorative: the adversary can steal the patron's key, exactly as in
// attach.spthy.  Without this rule the second disjunct could never be
// witnessed and we would not know the first disjunct is doing the work.
rule Compromise_Patron:
    [ !PatronKey($P, ~psk) ]
  --[ Compromised($P) ]->
    [ Out(~psk) ]


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HELPER INVARIANTS.  [reuse] makes these available to the proofs below;
// they carry the structural facts about a party's own record that the
// supersession lemmas otherwise have to rediscover at every step.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Every issuance is preceded by the patron's view being set to that key.
// Immediate: Issue_Currency consumes View, and only Learn_Subject and
// Receive_Adoption produce one, both stamping ViewIs.
lemma issuance_holds_the_view [reuse]:
  "All P S sk e #j. Issued(P, S, sk, e) @ #j ==> ViewHeld(P, S, sk) @ #j"

// A view never returns to a key it has been overwritten away from.  True
// because a successor is FRESH (see Recovery_Adoption): a key that already
// existed can never be the new key of a later adoption, so the successor
// relation has no cycles.
lemma view_never_returns [reuse, use_induction]:
  "All A S old new #r #v.
     ( Overwrote(A, S, old, new) @ #r
     & ViewIs(A, S, old) @ #v )
     ==> #v < #r"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SUPERSESSION PROPERTIES (design Section 12.6.5, ruled 2026-09-05)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// 1. NO FRESH ISSUANCE FOR A KEY THE PATRON HAS OVERWRITTEN.  This is the
//    cryptographically enforceable half: the patron is itself a horizon
//    member, so a recovery adoption replaces the binding in ITS records too,
//    and after that it has nothing to issue for.  Without this, a patron
//    could mint a brand-new attestation for a rotated-away key -- the gap the
//    fourth cross-family review found and the reason for this rebuild.
lemma no_issuance_after_overwrite [use_induction]:
  "All P S sk new e #r #j.
     ( Overwrote(P, S, sk, new) @ #r
     & Issued(P, S, sk, e) @ #j
     & #r < #j )
     ==> (Ex #k. Compromised(P) @ #k & #k < #j)"

// 2. WHAT A SUPERSEDED KEY CAN STILL SPEND.  Any acceptance of a key the
//    patron has since overwritten rests on an attestation issued BEFORE the
//    rotation.  So the window is bounded by that attestation's expiry and
//    cannot be extended -- design Section 12.6.5: "Expiry bounds what a
//    stolen credential can spend."  Together with 1, this is the whole of
//    what the protocol layer can enforce here.
lemma superseded_acceptance_predates_the_rotation [use_induction]:
  "All P S sk new e #i #r.
     ( Accepted(P, S, sk, e) @ #i
     & Overwrote(P, S, sk, new) @ #r )
     ==> ( (Ex #j. Issued(P, S, sk, e) @ #j & #j < #r)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(P) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

// 3. IGNORANCE STILL ACCEPTS, and it must, or the two lemmas above would be
//    holding vacuously.  A party outside the horizon never receives the
//    adoption, keeps its old view, and goes on accepting the old key until
//    the attestation expires.  design Section 12.6.5: that is the ignorance
//    the fail-open row is for, not a violation.  The trace below has a
//    rotation, an untouched second party, and an acceptance after it.
lemma ignorance_still_accepts_after_a_rotation:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S old new e #t #i.
       RotationExists(S, old, new) @ #t
     & Accepted(P, S, old, e) @ #i
     & #t < #i
     & not(Ex #k. Compromised(P) @ #k)"

end
